 "messages": [
  {
   "id": "p1bBc7-IJwAJ",
   "from": "Tobias Kockmann",
   "author": "b3c6794838d616e7",
   "date": "2016-04-28T21:44:23Z",
   "subject": "effect size statistics",
   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\">Dear MSstats team,<div><br></div><div>following the concepts shown in this Nature methods article</div><div><br></div><div><a href=\"http://www.nature.com/nmeth/journal/v12/n3/full/nmeth.3288.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=http://www.nature.com/nmeth/journal/v12/n3/full/nmeth.3288.html&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1793232174292000&amp;usg=AOvVaw3c7ZY2SrnQRJ1_XuCC9reb\">http://www.nature.com/nmeth/journal/v12/n3/full/nmeth.3288.html</a><br></div><div><br></div><div>I am trying to generate an effect size statistics using MSstats output. Unfortunately, I am a missing some crucial information which could be easily added to the current MSstats report, or is only a few clicks away, but the current column labels are not really precise, so I am not sure if my interpretations and calculations are correct. I created a two group comparison (let&#39;s call the exp. conditions A and B) and the MSstats report contains the following variables:</div><div><br></div><div>A vs. B, log2FC, SE, df, t.value, p.value and adj.p.value</div><div><br></div><div>What I nedd to understand is the following:</div><div><br></div><div>a) A vs. B is a label for the contrast I suppose. Which condition of this contrast was used as the base level in the lm(e)? A or B?</div><div>b) I assume in a 2group comparison the log2FC is my effect size estimate, correct?</div><div>c) SE is the standard error of the log2FC, meaning the std. error of my effect size estimate?</div><div>d) Which t.value is given here? How was it calculated (sig. level? 95%, one-tail? two-tails?) What is it referring to?</div><div>e) How was the p-value calculated? Is it coming from a post-hoc test? So in my case a two-sample t-test? Or was it calculated differently e.g. by simulation?</div><div><br></div><div>What I would like to do now is to calculate an 95% CI for my effect size estimate (log2FC). Is this formula correct?</div><div><br></div><div>low.95%.CI = log2FC-t.value(95%two-tailed t.value, df)*SE</div><div>high.95%.CI = log2FC+t.value(95%two-tailed t.value, df)*SE</div><div><br></div><div>What I realised is that the t.value given in the table is NOT the same as when I look into a t.distribution table for a two-tailed 95% t-value at df as given in the table. How come? Is there a handy way to plot effect size estimates and their 95%CIs for a large number of features (something like the vulcano plot of effect size statistics?)</div><div><br></div><div>Greetings,</div><div>Tobi</div><div><br></div><div><br></div></div>"
    ]
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "1S4O2D2pBQAJ",
   "from": "Tsung-Heng Tsai",
   "author": "085b00b73e467d88",
   "date": "2016-04-29T02:35:17Z",
   "subject": "Re: effect size statistics",
   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\">Please see the responses in-line:</div>"
    ],
    [
     2,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><br><br>On Thursday, April 28, 2016 at 5:44:23 PM UTC-4, Tobias Kockmann wrote:<blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0;margin-left:0.8ex;border-left:1px #ccc solid;padding-left:1ex\"><div dir=\"ltr\">Dear MSstats team,<div><br></div><div>following the concepts shown in this Nature methods article</div><div><br></div><div><a href=\"http://www.nature.com/nmeth/journal/v12/n3/full/nmeth.3288.html\" rel=\"nofollow\" target=\"_blank\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=http://www.nature.com/nmeth/journal/v12/n3/full/nmeth.3288.html&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1793232174294000&amp;usg=AOvVaw0zZo7U-egyhfMX6eQYrnD3\">http://www.nature.com/nmeth/journal/v12/n3/full/nmeth.3288.html</a><br></div><div><br></div><div>I am trying to generate an effect size statistics using MSstats output. Unfortunately, I am a missing some crucial information which could be easily added to the current MSstats report, or is only a few clicks away, but the current column labels are not really precise, so I am not sure if my interpretations and calculations are correct. I created a two group comparison (let&#39;s call the exp. conditions A and B) and the MSstats report contains the following variables:</div><div><br></div><div>A vs. B, log2FC, SE, df, t.value, p.value and adj.p.value</div><div><br></div><div>What I nedd to understand is the following:</div><div><br></div><div>a) A vs. B is a label for the contrast I suppose. Which condition of this contrast was used as the base level in the lm(e)? A or B?</div></div></blockquote><div><br></div></div>"
    ],
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><div>This depends on the contrast matrix you specify. For example, if you want to compare A-B, which means log(A)-log(B) and the same as log(A/B), set &#39;1&#39; for A and &#39;-1&#39; for B in the row of your contrast matrix.</div></div>"
    ],
    [
     2,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><div> </div><blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0;margin-left:0.8ex;border-left:1px #ccc solid;padding-left:1ex\"><div dir=\"ltr\"><div>b) I assume in a 2group comparison the log2FC is my effect size estimate, correct?</div></div></blockquote><div><br></div></div>"
    ],
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><div>Correct.</div></div>"
    ],
    [
     2,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><div> </div><blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0;margin-left:0.8ex;border-left:1px #ccc solid;padding-left:1ex\"><div dir=\"ltr\"><div>c) SE is the standard error of the log2FC, meaning the std. error of my effect size estimate?</div></div></blockquote><div><br></div></div>"
    ],
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><div>Correct.</div></div>"
    ],
    [
     2,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><div> </div><blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0;margin-left:0.8ex;border-left:1px #ccc solid;padding-left:1ex\"><div dir=\"ltr\"><div>d) Which t.value is given here? How was it calculated (sig. level? 95%, one-tail? two-tails?) What is it referring to?</div></div></blockquote><div><br></div></div>"
    ],
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><div>It&#39;s t-statistic: the difference between two group means (on log scale), divided by the standard error of the difference. <br></div></div>"
    ],
    [
     2,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><div> </div><blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0;margin-left:0.8ex;border-left:1px #ccc solid;padding-left:1ex\"><div dir=\"ltr\"><div>e) How was the p-value calculated? Is it coming from a post-hoc test? So in my case a two-sample t-test? Or was it calculated differently e.g. by simulation?</div></div></blockquote><div><br></div></div>"
    ],
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><div>It&#39;s calculated by comparing the test statistic against the t-distribution (two-tailed hypothesis is considered).</div></div>"
    ],
    [
     3,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><div> <br></div><blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0;margin-left:0.8ex;border-left:1px #ccc solid;padding-left:1ex\"><div dir=\"ltr\"><div><br></div><div>What I would like to do now is to calculate an 95% CI for my effect size estimate (log2FC). Is this formula correct?</div><div><br></div><div>low.95%.CI = log2FC-t.value(95%two-tailed t.value, df)*SE</div><div>high.95%.CI = log2FC+t.value(95%two-tailed t.value, df)*SE</div><div><br></div><div>What I realised is that the t.value given in the table is NOT the same as when I look into a t.distribution table for a two-tailed 95% t-value at df as given in the table. How come? Is there a handy way to plot effect size estimates and their 95%CIs for a large number of features (something like the vulcano plot of effect size statistics?)</div><div><br></div><div>Greetings,</div><div>Tobi</div><div><br></div><div><br></div></div></blockquote></div>"
    ]
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "WnBdgsWpBQAJ",
   "from": "Olga Vitek",
   "author": "2889aa2e61425c8e",
   "date": "2016-04-29T02:45:00Z",
   "subject": "Re: effect size statistics",
   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\">Dear Tobi,<div><br></div><div>to add to Tsung-Heng&#39;s comments:</div><div><br></div><div>- Not sure about the problem with the tables. For example, for a two-sided test at the significance level of 0.95, you need to look for the t-value such that the area under the Student distribution to the right of this value is 1-0.05/2. If this is difficult you can use the functions in R &#39;qt&#39; and &#39;pt&#39;.</div><div><br></div><div>- Please keep in mind that in the case of multiple proteins the p-value needs to be adjusted for multiple testing (see adj.p.value), and the level of the confidence interval also needs to be adjusted for multiplicity. The paper that you cite only focuses on one test and does not address the multiplicity.</div><div><br></div><div>HTH</div><div>Olga</div></div>"
    ]
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "3Wclq1JjBgAJ",
   "from": "Tobias Kockmann",
   "author": "b3c6794838d616e7",
   "date": "2016-05-01T11:25:15Z",
   "subject": "Re: effect size statistics",
   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\">Hi Olga,<div><br></div><div>thx very much for your quick answer. I will have a look at the R functions you suggested. I quickly did the t-value/CI calculation using Excel, but by using R code it might be easier to follow my calculations. Regarding your second point, how does CI adjustment work? Do I simply lower the initial alpha level (feels like the idea behind Bonferroni correction?) or is there something like controlling family-wise errors (balance specificity and sensitivity) for CIs?</div><div><br></div><div>How would you visualise effect size estimates and their corresponding CIs for n&lt;&lt;&lt;p problem (e.g 2000 features)?</div><div><br></div><div>Greetings,</div><div><br></div><div>Tobi</div></div>"
    ]
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "EdH2wllwBgAJ",
   "from": "Olga Vitek",
   "author": "2889aa2e61425c8e",
   "date": "2016-05-01T15:24:00Z",
   "subject": "Re: effect size statistics",
   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\">Dear Tobi,<div><br></div><div>The Bonferroni correction controls family-wise error rate, and is one way to do this. It involves dividing alpha by the number of confidence intervals. Another way is to use the approach by Benjamini and Hochberg, and control False Discovery rate. It is a different adjustment to alpha, see p. 298 of this manuscript <a href=\"http://www.stat.purdue.edu/~doerge/BIOINFORM.D/FALL06/Benjamini%20and%20Y%20FDR.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=http://www.stat.purdue.edu/~doerge/BIOINFORM.D/FALL06/Benjamini%2520and%2520Y%2520FDR.pdf&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1793232174295000&amp;usg=AOvVaw30hDR7EMEXk9N87JzRV-J4\">http://www.stat.purdue.edu/~doerge/BIOINFORM.D/FALL06/Benjamini%20and%20Y%20FDR.pdf</a>.</div><div><br></div><div>For visualization you can make a separate plot per protein (as it is done in MSstats), or overlay all the proteins in one plot.</div><div><br></div><div>HTH</div><div>Olga</div></div>"
    ]
   ]
  },
  {
